<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5/5/1/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "4"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5/5/1/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part20" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5/5/1/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "Subsection 39(2) relates to trade marks that may be rejected because they contain or consist of any of the signs prescribed in regulation 4.15 for the purposes of this subsection. The practice to be applied in deciding whether a ground for rejection exists under subsection 39(2) is given in detail in Part 31 of this manual. The practice aims at keeping in mind the presumption that trade marks are registrable unless there is a clear ground for rejection. The mere presence of a prescribed sign as part of a trade mark may not be sufficient reason to raise a ground for rejection, especially if it is unlikely that the presence of that sign will give rise to deception or confusion. Part 31 Prohibited and Prescribed Signs" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part20/5/5/1/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/upstreamRef> "TMM/Part20/5/5/1/2" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
