<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part29/5/5/4> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/hasCitation> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/cite/TMM/Part29/5/5/4/prov/1> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part29/5/5/4> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/hasCitation> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/cite/TMM/Part29/5/5/4/prov/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part29/5/5/4> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "29.5. International Non-Proprietary Names (generic names for pharmaceutical substances) and INN stems" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part29/5/5/4> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "5.4 Section 43 ground for rejection in relation to INNs" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part29/5/5/4> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "Part 29 Section 43 - Trade Marks likely to Deceive or Cause Confusion" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part29/5/5/4> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/onPage> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part29/5> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part29/5/5/4> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "5"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part29/5/5/4> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part29" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part29/5/5/4> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "A ground for rejection under section 43 exists where a trade mark, or part of a trade mark, to be used in relation to pharmaceuticals or veterinary substances is the same as, or may connote, a notified INN and use of the trade mark in respect of the goods covered by the specification are not restricted to the particular substance indicated by the INN. Use of such a trade mark would be likely to give rise to deception or confusion. Determining whether a non-identical trade mark connotes an INN will depend on the particular circumstances of the case. However, if a trade mark is an obvious derivation of an INN, a connotation is likely to exist. See Aventis Pharma SA v Alphapharm Pty Ltd [2005] ATMO 15 (TM ROXIMYCIN and INN ROXITHROMYCIN) and Sanofi-Aventis v Eremad Pty Ltd [2009] ATMO 97 (TM OXALATIN and INN OXALIPLATIN) where both were found to contain a connotation which would be likely to cause confusion or deception if the goods did not contain the drug specified in the relevant INN. A section 43 ground for rejection may be overcome by the applicant agreeing to a condition of registration limiting use of the trade mark to goods containing the substance indicated by the relevant INN. The endorsement suggested by the Registrar in these circumstances is: It is a condition of registration that any use in respect of pharmaceuticals will be limited to such goods containing the substance designated by the International Non-Proprietary Name <INN>. However such an endorsement is unlikely to assist in overcoming grounds for rejection which have been raised for other reasons (e.g. under section 41 or section 44 of the Trade Marks Act 1995)." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part29/5/5/4> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/upstreamRef> "TMM/Part29/5/5/4" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
