<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/11> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/contentHash> "sha256:62de45aa518cdd9583fe43b57c5d58544105bf59339b6e90dba5c1951d8d9844" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/11> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/hasCitation> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/cite/TMM/Part30/2/2/11/prov/0> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/11> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "2.11 Trade marks which must be regarded as scandalous" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/11> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "30.2. Scandalous signs" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/11> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "Part 30 Signs that are Scandalous and Use Contrary to Law" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/11> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/onPage> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/11> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "12"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/11> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part30" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/11> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "There are some trade marks which, no matter what the market is, will always be regarded as scandalous and therefore unacceptable to the Registrar. These trade marks are often those likely to be seen as promoting racial vilification, religious intolerance and personal abuse of an individual. The following examples demonstrate the kinds of phrases which will always attract a ground for rejection under section 42(a): trade marks with elements of personal abuse (eg ROOT YOU, <politician's name>); trade marks incorporating racial or ethnic abuse (eg F**K <country or ethnic group name>); trade marks incorporating abuse of a national flag (eg F**K with the Australian flag forming the stars); and trade marks incorporating elements of religious intolerance/abuse (eg ALL <name of religion>S ARE EVIL)." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/2/2/11> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/upstreamRef> "TMM/Part30/2/2/11" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
