<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3/3/1> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "2"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3/3/1> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part30" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3/3/1> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "At the examination stage the question of whether a trade mark is contrary to law is usually limited to circumstances where: the trade mark contains or consists of a sign the use of which is precluded under a particular piece of legislation e.g. Telstra Corporation Act 1991 (Cth), Olympic Insignia Protection Act 1987 (Cth), Wine Australia Act 2013 (Cth); or a court decision has been issued on the particular trade mark which establishes the illegality of its use." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3/3/1> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/upstreamRef> "TMM/Part30/3/3/1" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
