<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3/3/4> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/onPage> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3/3/4> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "5"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3/3/4> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part30" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3/3/4> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "Following the decision in Advantage Rent-a-Car Inc v Advantage Car Rental Pty Ltd [2001] FCA 683 (‘Advantage Rent-a-Car’) it became clear that the Registrar was obliged in certain circumstances to give consideration to legislation such as the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth) and the Trade Practices Act 1974 (Cth) (now the Competition and Consumer Act 2010 (Cth)). Consideration of these types of issues is unlikely to occur in the examination stages, but may become relevant during opposition proceedings. However, if an examiner is provided with definite information indicating that a particular trade mark would be contrary to any relevant legislation, a ground for rejection must be raised." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
