<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3/3/5/5~2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "30.3. Use contrary to law" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3/3/5/5~2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/headingPath> "Part 30 Signs that are Scandalous and Use Contrary to Law" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3/3/5/5~2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/onPage> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3/3/5/5~2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/ordinal> "18"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3/3/5/5~2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/partId> "Part30" <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part30/3/3/5/5~2> <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/text> "A number of emblems of the Australian Army, Royal Australian Air Force and Royal Australian Navy have been included on the Trade Marks database, and will be found as -9000000 signs during normal searching. Grounds for rejection should be raised under section 42(b) if one of these emblems, or something which could be taken as the emblem is included within the trade mark, and the applicant is not obviously entitled to use it. Written consent from the Minister will be required for the application to proceed. 3.5.7 Defence Regulation 2016, part 14 A trade mark containing or consisting of a term prohibited by the Defence Regulation 2016 (Cth) will attract grounds for rejection under section 42(b). In relation to the letters, grounds for rejection will only be raised where the letters appear as a recognisably separate element, rather than contained within a word where they do not retain a separate identity. For example, the prohibited letters ARA would give rise to grounds for rejection when they appear in the trade mark ARA-X, but not when they appear in the trade mark MARAUDER CARAVANS. Where a ground for rejection is raised, evidence of the Defence Minister's consent to the applicant's use of the term will be required before the trade mark can be accepted for registration. Annex A3 contains a list of prohibited terms relevant to the Defence Regulations, as well as some of the emblems. Note: It is only the use of the terms themselves that is prohibited, and not the use of terms that would be substantially identical or deceptively similar. For example, use of AUS DEFFENCE FORSE would not give rise to a section 42(b) objection based on the Regulation. Examiners should consider grounds for rejection under section 43 where use of similar words or letters would be likely to deceive or cause confusion due to indicating a connection with the Australian Defence Force. 3.5.8 Environment Protection and Biodiversity Conservation Act 1999 Regulation 12.38 of the Environment Protection and Biodiversity Conservation Regulations 2000 (Cth) (supporting the above Act) reads as follows: A person must not use a captured image of a Commonwealth reserve to derive commercial gain. For subregulation (1): captured image includes an image that was not captured for a commercial purpose or in contravention of the Act or these Regulations." <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/graph/source> .
